On Wed, Oct 15, 2025 at 6:54=E2=80=AFPM Takashi Iwai <tiwai@suse.de> wrote:
> Did you boot (Debian's) 6.17.2 kernel, copy its config from
> /proc/config.* to .config, then run "make localmodconfig"?
> If the problem is seen with it, we'd have to take a deeper look.

Yes! Here are more details:

- I installed Debian's kernel 6.17.2-1~exp1. Note that it is "experimental"
(this is even more unstable status than "unstable")
- I booted into it
- I noticed that audio works
- Here is config of that kernel: (attached "config-6.17.2-amd64")
- Then I downloaded sources of vanilla stable kernel 6.17.2 from kernel.org
- I applied Debian's patches to it. I took them from here:
https://salsa.debian.org/kernel-team/linux/-/blob/debian/6.17.2-1_exp1/debi=
an/patches/series?ref_type=3Dtags
- I copied Debian's config (see above) to .config (note: there is no
/proc/config*
in Debian kernels, there is /boot/config*)
- I run "make localmodconfig", then "make"
- I rebooted into this new kernel
- Here is its config: ("config-6.17.2-debdeb")
- Audio doesn't work

So, it seems Debian does some inexplicable magic when building its kernels.
Debian's kernel work, but attempting to reproduce what they do doesn't work=
.

Also: I'm totally okay with doing more experiments. Just say me what to do,
and I will do.

--
Askar Safin

